[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-49548"] :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{9\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/9)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-351834/field-1/41750b9e1f010bfc6f4245021deeec487f9a9f365d2f8174caf96720a7a0ab1b" :answer/id #uuid "6899a725-4d66-5761-b512-2fe14b6449c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351834/field-1/f6faf3e09627fa8c7ad1f8f5ed2f2556f1239251809c6893ce25024ff4cbb126" :answer/id #uuid "6f16e828-9dcf-58a5-b632-4ae0241bcc01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351834/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2a6b1b6-12a6-50d5-8c5d-8ceb4cdd7556" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351834/field-1/41750b9e1f010bfc6f4245021deeec487f9a9f365d2f8174caf96720a7a0ab1b" :answer-field/choices ["q-351834/field-1/41750b9e1f010bfc6f4245021deeec487f9a9f365d2f8174caf96720a7a0ab1b" "q-351834/field-1/f6faf3e09627fa8c7ad1f8f5ed2f2556f1239251809c6893ce25024ff4cbb126"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351834" :question/id #uuid "5586c350-c98f-5ffa-862c-03fe553a31e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-351834" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-75{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-75{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3\\cdot (1-25{x}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}{\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-25{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(5x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(5x)}^{2}.$   \n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-0.2$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-75{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(5x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\,\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\,\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-351834/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-351834"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-351839/field-1/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" :answer/id #uuid "73179cf7-0dc4-5e8f-8ada-b90c4dc580c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351839/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2038c937-5b46-5d3a-bbbf-7d9fa5c70ac3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351839/field-1/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" :answer-field/choices ["q-351839/field-1/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351839/field-2/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "82ba530d-ebc7-5100-b189-6ed20c074a7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351839/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "848e9332-aa52-5cf4-b8a7-44668b3f94fc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351839/field-2/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer-field/choices ["q-351839/field-2/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351839" :question/id #uuid "ba843db4-f516-5ba6-b64a-a5077c43f132" :question/math-academy-id "q-351839" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{12}{3+27{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡($\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{12}{3+27{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=4{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{1+9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $9{x}^{2}=(3x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $12$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{12}{3+27{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{1+(3x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{12}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{12}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(12)-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\,arctan\\,(12)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-351839/field-1" "q-351839/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-351839"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
